{
  "prog-pd-heading": "Built on Product Design Technology",
  "prog-disc-heading": "Specialise in Two of Six Indigenous Disciplines",
  "prog-intro": "Before any student touches clay, metal, leather, bamboo or wood at DIAT, they spend\n        dedicated time in Product Design, the foundational strand that runs across\n        all year groups from first year through to final year. This is not a separate discipline\n        but the intellectual and technical spine of the entire DIAT curriculum.",
  "prog-pd-card-title": "Product Design Technology",
  "prog-pd-desc": "Product Design at DIAT equips every student with Computer-Aided Design (CAD)\n          software skills and systematic design methodology before they begin production\n          in any craft discipline. Students learn to conceptualise, visualise, and\n          technically specify their intended artworks digitally, producing detailed\n          CAD drawings and 3D models of pieces before commencing physical production.",
  "prog-pd-desc2": "This CAD-to-craft approach bridges indigenous tradition with contemporary\n          design technology, producing graduates who can communicate their work\n          professionally to clients, galleries, and global markets, a capability\n          that directly underpins DIAT's commercial art services arm and the\n          international competitiveness of its graduates.",
  "disc-product-intro1": "Product Design is not one discipline among six at DIAT — it is the\n        intellectual and technical spine that holds all six together. Every student,\n        regardless of their chosen craft specialisation, studies Product Design from\n       first year through to final year. Before any student throws clay, cuts leather,\n        welds metal, carves wood or lattes bamboo, they have already designed what they     intend to make digitally, precisely, and professionally.",
  "disc-product-intro2": "At DIAT, Product Design means learning to use Computer-Aided Design (CAD)\n        software to produce technical drawings, 3D models, and rendered visualisations\n        of craft objects before physical production begins. Students learn to write design\n        briefs, specify materials, calculate dimensions, and present their concepts to\n        a professional standard, the skills that allow a DIAT graduate to walk\n        into any client meeting, gallery, or design competition and communicate their\n        work with authority.",
  "comm-hero-sub": "DIAT's academic work does not end in the studio. Through a structured three-month internship programme, students are embedded with artisan communities across Ghana learning, documenting and formally attributing the indigenous craft knowledge that sustains the department.",
  "home-about-1": "Founded in 1976 as a Diploma programme, the then Department of Integrated Rural Art and Industry (IRAI), now the Department of Indigenous Art and Technology (DIAT) at the Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and Technology (KNUST) is Ghana's pioneering institution for indigenous art education, uniquely combining Academia, commercial craft production, and active Community engagement under one roof.",
  "home-about-2": "Our structured curriculum is centered around six core disciplines with Product Design as the base. Clay and Earthenware, Fibres and Fabrics, Leather Design and Technology, Metal Production and Fabrication, Rattan and Bamboo Processing, and Wood and Furniture. ",
  "about-hero-sub": "From a Diploma programme founded in 1976 to Ghana's premier academic institution\n      for indigenous art and craft education, the story of DIAT is the story of\n      a nation's heritage reclaimed, formalised, and carried forward.",
  "about-para-1": "Founded in 1976 as a Diploma programme, the then Department of Integrated Rural Art and\n          Industry (IRAI), now the Department of Indigenous Art and Technology (DIAT) at the\n          Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and Technology (KNUST) is Ghana's pioneering\n          institution for indigenous art education, uniquely combining Academia, commercial craft\n          production, and active Community engagement under one roof.",
  "about-para-2": "Operating under the College of Art and Built Environment - CABE at the Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and Technology - KNUST, DIAT has for five decades served as the foremost academic custodian of Ghana's indigenous craft traditions from  Clay and Earthenware, Fibres and Fabrics, Leather Design and Technology, Metal Production and Fabrication, Rattan and Bamboo Processing, and Wood and Furniture. The department's mandate is not merely to preserve these traditions but to innovate within them, equipping each generation of practitioners with both the cultural knowledge and the contemporary design tools needed to carry indigenous craft into the global marketplace.\nThrough project-based learning, students in each of the six (6) disciplines receive the necessary entrepreneurial training and skills to create artworks that incorporate with at least one of DIAT's philosophies: indigenous concepts, integration concepts, material integration, or technology integration of one or more of the six (6) disciplines. The department then preserves and markets these artworks for public consumption. ",
  "about-para-3": "Our structured curriculum is centered around six core disciplines with Product Design as the base. Clay and Earthenware, Fibres and Fabrics, Leather Design and Technology, Metal Production and Fabrication, Rattan and Bamboo Processing, and Wood and Furniture programmes ground every student in Product Design from first year, equipping them with Computer-Aided Design (CAD) tools and design methodology before guiding them into mastery of two of six indigenous craft disciplines. This CAD-to-craft approach designing digitally before making by hand is a major part of what makes DIAT graduates uniquely competitive: they are simultaneously cultural custodians and contemporary design professionals.",
