  "about-ann-h2": "50 Years of<br><em>Indigenous Art & Technology</em> Education",
  "about-ann-body": "DIAT marks half a century of preserving, formalising, and promoting\n          Ghana’s indigenous craft heritage within one of Africa’s foremost technical universities.",
  "about-photo-diat-building-title": "Department Compound",
  "about-hist-body": "The story of the Department of Indigenous Art and Technology (DIAT) at Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and Technology (KNUST) is one of vision, resilience, and transformation. Founded in 1976 as the Integrated Rural Art and Industry program, its original mission was clear: to harness Ghana’s rich indigenous art traditions and channel them into sustainable rural industries. At a time when modernisation threatened to overshadow traditional crafts, the department served as a guardian of heritage, ensuring that weaving, carving, dyeing, and other indigenous practices were not only preserved but also reimagined for contemporary relevance.\nIn its early years, the department offered a diploma program that focused on practical skills for rural artisans and students. The emphasis was on cottage industries (small-scale enterprises) that could empower communities economically while safeguarding cultural identity. Students were trained to become both artisans and entrepreneurs, capable of producing crafts that embodied Ghanaian tradition while meeting the demands of modern markets.\nBy the 1990s, the department recognised that indigenous art could not remain static. It began to integrate technology and design innovation, preparing students to navigate a rapidly globalising creative economy. This evolution culminated in 1999 when the diploma program was upgraded to a degree program, marking a significant academic milestone. The transition reflected not only the department’s growing reputation but also its alignment with national development goals by producing graduates who could lead in both cultural preservation and industrial innovation.\nThe new millennium brought further transformation. The department rebranded as the Department of Indigenous Art and Technology (DIAT), a name that captured its dual commitment: safeguarding indigenous knowledge while embracing technological advancement. Graduate programs—MPhil and PhD—were introduced, positioning DIAT as a hub for advanced research in indigenous art-tech innovation, sustainable textiles, and cultural entrepreneurship. This academic expansion allowed DIAT to contribute to global discussions on sustainability, heritage, and creative industries while remaining deeply rooted in Ghanaian traditions.\nBeyond academia, DIAT has had a profound impact on community empowerment. Through apprenticeship models, cooperative organisations, and rural outreach, the department has linked artisans to modern markets, fostered fair-trade practices, and nurtured community resilience. Its graduates have established creative enterprises, revitalised endangered crafts, and championed cultural education. In doing so, DIAT has become more than just an academic department; it is a movement that bridges tradition and modernity, rural and urban, local and global.\nToday, DIAT embodies cultural resilience by preserving Ghana’s artistic heritage against the forces of globalisation. It demonstrates academic innovation by integrating indigenous knowledge with modern design, sustainability, and digital technology. Moreover, it advances community empowerment by equipping artisans and graduates to transform their communities through creativity and entrepreneurship.\nThis journey from a rural art diploma in 1976 to a globally relevant academic department justifies the celebration of DIAT. Its wealth is not measured in material terms alone but in cultural capital, academic excellence, and social impact. DIAT stands as a testament to Ghana’s ability to honour its past while shaping its future, making its celebration both necessary and inspiring.",
  "about-tl1-body": "Founded in 1976 as the Integrated Rural Art and Industry program, its original mission was clear: to harness Ghana’s rich indigenous art traditions and channel them into sustainable rural industries.",
  "about-tl2-year": "1990",
  "about-tl2-body": "The department began to integrate technology and design innovation, preparing students to navigate a rapidly globalising creative economy. ",
  "about-tl2-title": "Integration Concept Established",
  "about-tl3-year": "1999",
  "about-tl3-body": "The diploma program was upgraded to a degree program.\nIn 2022, The department rebranded as the Department of Indigenous Art and Technology (DIAT). Graduate programs MPhil and PhD were introduced, positioning DIAT as a hub for advanced research in indigenous art-tech innovation, sustainable materials, and cultural entrepreneurship. ",
  "about-tl3-title": "Upgraded from Diploma to a Degree Program ",
  "about-who-p1": "The programme emphasizes the concept of integration, material sustainability, indigenous design concepts and technology interpreted in contemporary sense. To achieve this, selected projects are done to demonstrate the creative skills of the students and the viability of integrating different materials and techniques to produce specific items, their handling, preservation and marketing.\nOver the years, the programme has turned out graduates who have had impact on industry and society in various ways. The impact includes the recognition of local raw material for integrated indigenous art, design and development of appropriate technology to support indigenous art industries, the production of functional and aesthetic products that are based on professional competence, managers for local industries, and personnel adept at the teaching of integrated indigenous art forms.\n",
  "about-who-p2": "In 2009, proposals for the running of master’s programmes (Master of Integrated Art and Industry and Master of Philosophy in Integrated Art) were developed. Approval to run the master’s programmes was given in 2011 and led to the admission of the first batch of master’s students in the 2012-2013 academic year.\n",
  "about-tl4-body": "Half a century of indigenous art and tech education, marked by this digital platform and a year of events.",
  "about-tl4-year": "2026 and Beyond",
  "about-hero-intro": "DIAT is one of the few university departments in Ghana dedicated entirely to indigenous \n              art craft and technology innovation taught not as cultural history but as living, professionally viable creative practices.",
  "site-footer-sub": "KNUST · College of Art and Built Environment CABE · Kumasi, Ghana",
  "home-hero-h1": "Ghana's<br>\n          <em class=\"dept-hero__title-accent\">Indigenous Art and Technology</em><br>\n          Shown to the World",
  "prog-hero-body": "DIAT offers the Bachelor of Art in Integrated Rural Art and Industry (B.A. IRAI) degree across six indigenous craft disciplines and an MPhil in Integrated Art, all underpinned by Product Design.",
  "prog-overview-h2": "About the Programme",
  "prog-overview-p1": "Your Academic Journey from Foundation to Expertise.\nOur undergraduate curriculum is designed like a funnel. You start wide with broad exploration and gradually narrow your focus, graduating as a highly specialized professional.\n\nFirst year: Exploration\n\nYou will build a strong foundation by exploring six distinct disciplines, giving you a well-rounded introduction to the entire creative and technological field including Product Design and Communication Skills. There are SIX areas of specialisation (Rattan and Bamboo, Woodwork, Clay and Earthenware, Leather Technology, Fibres and Fabrics, and Metal Production and Fabrication) in the programme.\n\nSecond year: Refinement\nThe narrowing process begins. You will drop two disciplines to focus intensely on four core areas, while expanding your mindset with crucial complementary courses like Product Design, African Arts and Culture, and Entrepreneurship Skills.  At Year Two, the student specialises in any of the four disciplines.\n\nThird year and Final year: Specialization & Practice\n At Year Three and Four, the student majors in any two of the four. Your final year is all about mastery. You will fully specialize in your final two chosen disciplines as stated and apply your knowledge to the real world through an Extended Research Project allowing you to dive deep into an industry topic you are passionate about.\n",
  "prog-overview-p2": "In Years 1 and 2, MPhil Integrated Art candidates are allowed to choose any one of the six core disciplines in addition to the research Methodology, sustainability and integration and Product Design.",
  "about-tri1-b": "Six craft disciplines taught to professional standard, underpinned by Product Design - CAD methodology across all four years. When it comes to the academic services, (DIAT’s first 1st Arm), the Department of Indigenous Art and Technology (DIAT) has a structured curriculum centered around six core disciplines with Product Design as the base. They are, Clay and Earthenware, Fibres and Fabrics, Leather Design and Technology, Metal Production and Fabrication, Rattan and Bamboo Processing, and Wood and Furniture. Through project-based learning, students in each of the six (6) disciplines receive the necessary entrepreneurial training and skills to create artworks that incorporate with at least one of the following philosophies: indigenous concepts, integration concepts, material integration, or technology integration of one or more of the six (6) disciplines. The department then preserves and markets these artworks for public consumption. ",
  "about-tri3-b": "Three-month nationwide internship embeds students with artisan communities, formally attributing indigenous craft knowledge. This three-month initiative affords students the opportunity to acquire firsthand experience with traditional crafting techniques and cultural practices, bridging academic learning, technology, and real-world application. Upon returning, students create innovative and practically functional artworks that reflect their deep understanding of both traditional craftsmanship and contemporary design. Some of these artworks are advertised as prototypes at the department for public consumption while promoting the department simultaneously.",
  "about-tri2-b": "Commissioned artwork, bespoke craft production, and design services offered to individual, corporate, and institutional clients. Commercial art services like marketing of artworks, Art consultancy, commissioned Art and installation services are some of the services the department renders to the public.",
