  "home-about-p1": "The then Department of Integrated Rural Art and Industry was set up in 1976 as a Diploma in Rural Art and Industry programme until 1998 when the Diploma programme was phased out in compliance with the dictates of the Government’s education reform policy. This led to its upgrade in 1999 to a degree programme (BA Integrated Rural Art and Industry), which has run to date. Its purpose has been to salvage and promote the indigenous art and crafts of the traditional Ghanaian heritage; to train students to acquire professional skills in designing, production and marketing Ghanaian artistic products that combine practical function with contemporary aesthetics, by the use of local raw materials; enhancing traditional technology and to equip students with professional entrepreneurial skills for the rural industry of Ghana. ",
  "home-about-p2": "The programme has six main disciplines with Product Design as the base. They are Clay and Earthenware; Fibres and Fabrics; Leather Design and Technology; Metal Production and Fabrication; Rattan and Bamboo Processing; and Wood and Furniture. ",
  "comm-partner-h2": "Practical Training, Industrial Attachment, Internship and Field Trips",
  "comm-partner-p1": "For practical training purposes, the core courses are structured such that fifty percent of all credits are dedicated to various practical lessons in the respective material areas. This is done through practical demonstrations to students and through pre-designed experiments and exploration by students.\nIn the second semester of Third Year, students undergo industrial internship which is supervised by both industry and the Department. This serves as a means for mentorship and acquisition of field experience for students. The industrial internship lays emphasis on acquisition of industrial skills and knowledge as well as updating industry with current outputs of research. Prior to this mandatory internship, students are given opportunities to embark on industrial attachments during their vacations right from year one to three. The benefits of this are that students get rich industrial experiences that enhance their academic outputs and make them better placed to select their areas for specialization in the programme.",
  "comm-partner-p2": "Placement for internship is according to interest and/or areas of specialization of the student under the auspices of an internship committee set by the Department. Assessment of the internship is done in three segments: student evaluation of industry, industry evaluation of student, and Department evaluation of student. Simultaneously, the respective host entities are evaluated by the Department in line with current trends and conformity to the standards set by the Department.\nAdditionally, the programme embarks on field trips under two approaches. The first is Department organized which takes place in the first and third years; the second, Section organized. The cardinal intention is two pronged: to give students the necessary exposure needed plus whipping up their interest in the programme of study. The Department organized version is mandatory and takes place in the second semester of the first year and first semester of the third year; whiles the section organized versions are at the discretion of the respective sections in the Department.\n",
  "comm-stat3": "Partner: Mastercard Foundation, AID to Atisans Ghana - ATAG"
}